it's great to welcome to the program  today Alexandra Nadeau Poff who is  professor of law at the University of  California Irvine also author of the  book punishment without crime how our  massive misdemeanor system traps the  innocent and makes America more unequal  Alexandra so great to have you on I  appreciate it thanks so much for having  me  so maybe to begin with let's talk about  misdemeanor offenses there is sometimes  the not inaccurate understanding in the  public that being charged with a  misdemeanor is less serious than being  charged with a felony which is of course  true in terms of the potential jail and  prison time that can come with it but  what you argue in the book is that there  is a whole system around and surrounding  misdemeanor offenses that has this  disproportionate impact on people who  are charged let's start there how does  it all work and how should we understand  the idea of misdemeanor offenses so  you're absolutely right they  misdemeanors are by definition less  serious than felonies that's how we  define misdemeanor and felony in our  criminal system it's worth remembering  that our felony system is  internationally infamous for being harsh  and punitive we lock up more people in  this country than any other nation on  the planet both in terms of absolute  numbers and percentages so our mass  incarceration has increased and and and  harsh and the way we handle felonies and  I think that's in many ways overshadowed  the profound harshness of our  misdemeanor system our misdemeanor  system is enormous it comprises eighty  percent of American criminal dockets  this is mostly what our criminal system  does and a misdemeanor technically  legally speaking is an offense for which  a person can serve no more than one year  incarceration even that definition is a  little broad there are two three four  year misdemeanors but the basic idea is  that there is an enormous world of  low-level offenses for which people are  and  punished less harshly than felonies but  there are millions of them and we are  punishing people often quite harshly for  their entire lives based on low-level  conduct based on minor crimes minor  behavior disorder crimes low-level theft  crimes sometimes things we don't even  think of his crimes loitering and  spitting spitting and jaywalking and  this is the bulk of what our criminal  system does to millions of people every  year so let's talk a little bit about  felony versus misdemeanor and then  misdemeanor versus nothing right so we  often read about particular cases where  someone is accused of having committed a  crime we read they could have been  charged with a felony but instead either  through an agreement or prosecutorial  discretion they were charged with a  misdemeanor instead you're still talking  about a case you're still talking about  a sort of court proceeding that's going  to have to be adjudicated in one way or  another when we talk about misdemeanor  or nothing at all we are now having a  sort of much more binary discussion  about is someone going to now be in the  system potentially requiring legal  assistance potentially on probation for  years at a time what will the impact the  unemployment so the difference between  misdemeanor or nothing seems to be one  we're selective application of the law  or discriminatory enforcement has this  all-or-nothing sort of impact in a sense  I think that's right the way to think  most I mean misdemeanors in your terms  to all or nothing quality of it is that  we've created an enormous net that  sweeps people up not for that original  felony that might get pled down to a  misdemeanor but for a very low level  conduct in the first place for loitering  for trespassing for spitting for  jaywalking for low-level thefts there  are aspects of course of our misdemeanor  system that do reach out and touch more  serious conduct  think of domestic violence is the  paradigmatic serious misdemeanor DUI of  course is a serious offense but from  many many millions of these cases people  are being brought into the criminal  system initially not because they are  particularly dangerous or harmful or bad  or scary as in the way they are brought  in for more serious felony offenses but  because they have committed level  conduct that we have gotten into the  habit of treating as a crime and it is  that decision the decision to  criminalize so much low-level conduct to  expand our criminal system in this  profoundly sweeping way that that the  book asks us to reconsider what can you  tell us in terms of the data around what  we would call I guess these less serious  misdemeanors and you can tell us what  you sort of include in that whether as  you mentioned its loitering jaywalking  etc what do we know about the unfair way  that that may be applied to either poor  people non-white people whatever sort of  discriminatory application you've  identified how can we quantify that yeah  that's a great question because we  actually have so little data about the  misdemeanor system it's many of these  courts many of these dockets operate a  little bit under the radar we don't keep  very good track of them for the book I  did new research around the country to  come to the estimate the best estimate  that we now have that there are  approximately 13 million misdemeanor  cases filed every year in this country  when I collected that data I asked every  state for their misdemeanor dockets and  for who is in those misdemeanor doctors  who are being who's being swept up how  are those offenses being enforced what  offenses make up those dockets and most  states didn't give me that information  some states told me that they that their  data systems were too outdated to  collect it or that they weren't willing  to make that data public I  we have spotty data around the country  that indicates in much the same way as  we now know about mass incarceration  that many of these low-level offenses  are disproportionately enforced against  the poor and against people of color and  we know that because of the nature of  the policing so one of the things that I  discuss in the book and a chapter  entitled money is how our misdemeanor  system as we say as we put it  criminalizes poverty it goes after poor  people almost by definition so many  misdemeanors are crimes of poverty it is  a many people drive for example on a  suspended license their license is  suspended because they couldn't pay a  ticket a fine or a fee wealthy people  well resource people will never be  charged with that kind of offence  homeless people of course are a famous  example they they're their crimes are  crimes of poverty sleeping and living  and existing on the street and so the we  use the low level system the low level  criminal system and many ways to sweep  up people based on their inability to  pay and then of course as has become  infamous after Ferguson sometimes we  lock them up after they've been charged  with a crime when they can't pay the  fines and fees that we have imposed upon  them for the for the original offense  and by that same token low-level  policing and low-level misdemeanor  conduct often sweeps up people of color  in part because we heavily police those  communities more heavily for these  low-level con low-level offenses so  people in those neighborhoods are more  likely to encounter the misdemeanor  system and then we see in jurisdictions  all over the country that offenses order  offenses like disorderly conduct and  loitering and jaywalking are  disproportionately enforced against  people of color and so that net if you  will that misdemeanor net is in many  ways the first step in creating the  racial disparities we see throughout our  criminal system so let's talk a little  bit about the jail time  opponent it wouldn't be unreasonable as  someone observing this system to think  that the top priority for anyone charged  with a misdemeanor would be to avoid  jail time just period right at whatever  option is available to avoid jail time  that's what you do you write in the book  that this is often not the case can you  talk a little bit about what the  circumstances are that lead to that and  why someone might accept jail time in  certain situations where they are  charged with misdemeanors yes so it  turns out that the punishment calculus  the calculations that individual people  make about about their choices in  cutting a plea deal and accepting a plea  deal are very complicated so for some  people avoiding jail time as you point  out is all-important they have children  they have a job they're afraid that if  they remain incarcerated they will lose  things that are vital importance to them  and so one of the things we see is that  many people will plead guilty rather  than contest their cases particularly if  they can't afford bail even when they  may be innocent or mean even when they  may have meritorious legal issues that  they could pursue they'd they'd rather  get out of jail as quickly as possible  and so they will accept wrongful  convictions or unfair punishment as the  price as the price of their freedom and  conversely and this was one of the  lessons I learned when I was a federal  and assistant federal public defender in  Baltimore many years ago for some people  the the long-term engagement with the  criminal system supervision and  probation the the now onerous fines and  fees that many people that are imposed  on many people when they are being  supervised supervision fees drug testing  fees tether fees electronic monitoring  fees over the months over the years of  supervision can  to an enormous burden being on probation  is deeply intrusive your house can be  searched you have to check in with your  probation officer you can't change jobs  you often can't leave your your  jurisdiction and so for many people they  would rather suffer through the the  burden and the weight of being  incarcerated for a briefer period of  time rather than turning their lives and  their in their liberty and their privacy  over to the state for a long period of  time so we've been talking about a lot  of the problems with the way the system  exists currently of course you pointed  out that there are misdemeanors that are  serious you talked about drunk driving  you talked about domestic violence and  many others  so what reforms do you believe should be  made to the system such that there is  not this idea that people who are  actually committing crimes that should  not be ignored  will still potentially face consequences  but we can fix a lot of the problems  that you identify yeah I think that's  the right way to think about it we have  this enormous system that's incredibly  important we need a functional  misdemeanor system that can respond  properly and fairly illegally to low  level harms and we have we have  overwhelmed that we fill it with chump  change  cases cases that might not have been  brought in the first place  if for example courts and probation  offices and prosecutorial offices and  sheriff's departments were not reliant  on the revenue source from those  low-level offenses in fact we have  created dysfunctional incentives making  it rational for many criminal justice  institutions to want to expand the  misdemeanor system to include many  low-level offenses to the detriment of  the integrity of the entire system and  as you say in the book I I make a number  of suggestions about the kinds of  reforms we can engage in they are not  pie-in-the-sky reforms they are being  engaged in it  many jurisdictions around the country  actually in in in cities and towns and  states in a kind of retail retail way  these but the take away the heart of the  matter is that we can shrink the system  that we do not need to be indeed we  should not be using our low level  criminal system this is vitally  important public institution on which we  all rely to raise money to be a revenue  source for criminal justice institutions  we shouldn't use it to criminalize  poverty we have better tools at our  disposal through the welfare system to  handle people who are suffering from  homelessness who have mental health  issues who have substance abuse problems  I to maintain the to enforce  gentrification boundaries well many  times we use low-level order maintenance  policing to clear coroner's or to  control the appearance of neighborhoods  not because these offenses are are  harmful not because these people are  criminals but because because  neighborhoods are gentrifying or  downtown's are gentrifying and these are  ways of enforcing those economic  boundaries the argument is that the  low-level criminal system is a crude and  poor and harsh tool for accomplishing  many of these public policy work on many  of them worthy public policy goals but  we have gone overboard in using this  particular tool and we should roll it  back yeah sort of the use of an axe when  maybe a scalpel is more appropriate to  use a kind of tired tired metaphor the  book is called yeah the the book is  called punishment without crime how our  massive misdemeanor system traps the  innocent and makes America more unequal  we've been speaking with the book's  author Alexander nano puff professor of  law at UC Irvine thank you so much for  talking to me today  thanks so much for having

